      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Quayholt Insurance, a fictional company. First you're on the red team: put together a fake Quayholt sign-in page from ready-made parts, choose the web address it would sit at, and send one email carrying its link. Nothing is built for real and nothing is collected. Then you swap seats and check five pages that Quayholt staff reached from links in their inbox. Only the pages are scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it lands on you",
        "flipIntro": "This is the page you put together, as Hana Novak, the claims handler you sent it to, would meet it after following the link. The parts picked out below are what make it believable.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "Hana Novak, the claims handler you sent it to, opened the link between two claims. The page wore the Quayholt look, so she began typing her password. That isn't carelessness: the page was built to pass for the real sign-in, and only the address in the bar gave it away.",
        "flipNoSend": "Your page didn't go out this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, the same kind of page turns up behind a link in a Quayholt inbox, and you're the one deciding whether to sign in.",
        "flipMissed": "The defence is the same either way: next, the same kind of page turns up behind a link in a Quayholt inbox, and you're the one deciding whether to sign in.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "The page passed without a slip this time, nothing in it to pull apart. Next, the same kind of page turns up behind a link in a Quayholt inbox, and you're the one deciding whether to sign in.",
        "noBlameLine": "A miss here is no reflection on you. Every one of these pages was built to pass for the real thing, and signing in to one says nothing about how careful a person is.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: before you type a password, read the address bar. Your organisation's real sign-in page sits on its own domain, and your password manager knows it. If a page asks you to sign in and the address is new to you, close it, go to the site the way you normally would, and report the link using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These pages were built to pass for the real sign-in, and anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "flipPageIntro": "This is the sign-in page you built, as Hana Novak, the claims handler you sent it to, would meet it after following the link. Anything that would give it away is picked out below.",
        "defendIntro": "Now you're the one reading your inbox. Five pages are open, each from a link. For each one, decide whether you'd sign in, then tick what you noticed."
